cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "serum-ca-cryo: Computational model for post-thaw calcium deficit in cryostored serum QC standards"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2025-04-26"
license: MIT
url: "https://github.com/tohafrit/serum-ca-cryo"
repository-code: "https://github.com/tohafrit/serum-ca-cryo"
abstract: >
  Open-source, reproducible computational model of the reversible post-thaw calcium
  deficit in cryostored human-serum quality-control standards. Cryoconcentration on
  freezing drives strong calcium-phosphate supersaturation (SI(HAp) ≈ +7.5,
  cross-checked against the WATEQ activity model); amorphous calcium phosphate
  nucleates on the glass surface and — because the viscous cryoprotected pool
  suppresses crystalline ripening — stays amorphous. A quiescent freshly-thawed draw
  under-samples this surface-bound deposit, and mixing reverses it. Stochastic
  nucleation accounts for the vial-to-vial and batch-to-batch variability. Two
  process-only fixes: prevent it by deep-frozen (vitrified, ≤ −80 °C) storage that
  arrests nucleation, or neutralize it with a defined mixing/re-suspension step at
  thaw. All figures and data tables are reproducible via `make all`.
